---
title: Build your fieldbook
lang: en
---

## Choose what to carry

By the end of this lesson, you can assemble a fieldbook that supports route visits and name the limits of its instructions.

This lesson has no narration. Work at your own reading pace. Bring together your route map, signal notes and handoff entry. These pieces describe the invented west route. They do not provide rules for a real marsh, workplace or emergency.

A [fieldbook]{A keeper's personal collection of route sketches, signal notes and visit reminders.} helps you find the information you already checked. It cannot replace a fresh station instruction.

--- Arrange your pages

## Arrange your pages

Put the route sketch first so you can find the station name before recording a visit. Keep clear, amber and covered meanings together on a second page. Mark invented practice signals as practice.

Choose a handoff entry that names a visit and carries an unresolved question. Preserve any correction. Put this entry after the signal notes so you can find it during a handoff.

Try reading the fieldbook with one hand covering the page titles. If a page becomes hard to identify, add a useful label rather than more decoration.

--- Check the fieldbook limit

## Check the fieldbook limit

Your fieldbook says clear permits a routine reply. You arrive at a covered lantern with no fresh instruction. The fieldbook describes the meaning of clear. It cannot supply the current instruction at this covered station.

Write two questions you would ask the route keeper before you act. Explain what your fieldbook already tells you and what you still need to establish. This is a reflection with no answer key.

Compare your questions with a partner. Where did each of you draw the boundary between a saved convention and a current station instruction?

--- Leave a question for tomorrow

## Leave a question for tomorrow

Review the fieldbook you assembled. Choose one page that another keeper could use without your explanation. Choose another that still depends on something you remember. Revise the second page.

Write a final reflection about an unresolved question from the course. Say what information would answer it and where you would look for that information on the invented route.

Keep this question with the fieldbook. A useful record makes uncertainty visible while preserving the instruction that is current.
